<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-changing-the-subject" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Changing the Subject</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.5 Formulae and Variations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Rewrite a formula to change the subject in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To allow learners to collaboratively discover the process of isolating a variable using a familiar linear budgeting formula and test their results empirically.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Circulate the room while groups discuss the workshop budget context. Ensure learners are correctly identifying how the fixed fee (<m>F</m>) and participant costs (<m>p \times n</m>) combine to form the total cost (<m>C</m>). Prompt them to think about how they would visually or algebraically "undo" these costs to figure out the number of students (<m>n</m>).
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>


<activity>
    <title>Changing the Subject of a Formula</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Imagine you are organizing a STEM workshop. The total cost (<m>C</m>) is calculated by a fixed venue fee (<m>F</m>) plus the cost per participant (<m>p</m>) multiplied by the number of students (<m>n</m>). The formula is:
        </p>

        <md>C = F + pn</md>

        <p>
            If you know your total budget and want to know how many students you can afford to host, you need to make <m>n</m> the subject.
        </p>

        <p><term>Exercise:</term></p>

        <p>
            Assume your venue cost <m>F = 5,000</m> KES, your total budget <m>C = 20,000</m> KES, and the cost per student <m>p = 500</m> KES. Calculate <m>n</m>.
        </p>

        <p><term>Challenge:</term></p>

        <p>
            Test what happens to <m>n</m> if the cost per student (<m>p</m>) decreases.
        </p>

    </statement>
</activity>

   <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the concepts and properties learners discovered in the workshop activity.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Highlight how groups worked backwards to isolate the number of students (<m>n</m>) from the total cost formula <m>C = F + pn</m>. Point out that finding <m>n</m> required subtracting the fixed venue fee (<m>F</m>) first, and then dividing the remaining budget by the cost per participant (<m>p</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Formalizing Inverse Operations:</alert> Explicitly link these steps to the rule of applying inverse operations in reverse BOMDAS/Order of Operations. Explain that when rewriting a formula to change the subject, we "undo" operations in reverse order—handling addition/subtraction before undoing multiplication/division.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <statement>
            <p><term>Changing the Subject of a Formula</term></p>
            <p>Changing the subject means rearranging a formula to make a chosen variable stand alone on one side of the equals sign.</p>
            <p>Use inverse operations (add/subtract, multiply/divide, square/square root) in reverse order of operations to isolate the variable.</p>
            </statement>
    </insight>

    <p>To isolate the number of students (<m>n</m>) from the workshop cost formula:</p>
            <md>n = \frac{C - F}{p}</md>
    <p>Always apply the exact same operation to both sides of the equation to keep it perfectly balanced.</p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

   <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In physics, the final velocity (<m>v</m>) of a constantly accelerating object can be determined using its initial velocity (<m>u</m>), acceleration rate (<m>a</m>), and the displacement distance (<m>s</m>) through the equation:
            </p>
            <md>v^2 = u^2 + 2as</md>
            <ol>
                <li>Make the displacement distance (<m>s</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                <li>Find <m>s</m> when <m>v = 10</m>, <m>u = 2</m>, and <m>a = 6</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <md>v^2 = u^2 + 2as</md>
                    <md>v^2 - u^2 = 2as</md>
                    <md>s = \frac{v^2 - u^2}{2a}</md>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <md>s = \frac{10^2 - 2^2}{2(6)}</md>
                    <md>s = \frac{100 - 4}{12}</md>
                    <md>s = \frac{96}{12}</md>
                    <md>s = 8</md>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The total perimeter (<m>P</m>) of a rectangle is calculated by doubling the sum of its length (<m>l</m>) and its width (<m>w</m>). The formula is given by:
            </p>
            <md>P = 2(l + w)</md>
            <ol>
                <li>Make the width (<m>w</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                <li>Find <m>w</m> when <m>P = 40</m> and <m>l = 12</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <md>P = 2(l + w)</md>
                    <md>\frac{P}{2} = l + w</md>
                    <md>w = \frac{P}{2} - l</md>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <md>w = \frac{40}{2} - 12</md>
                    <md>w = 20 - 12</md>
                    <md>w = 8</md>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In coordinate geometry, a straight line can be mapped out using a gradient/slope (<m>m</m>) and a vertical y-intercept (<m>c</m>) to locate any specific point coordinates (<m>x</m>, <m>y</m>). The linear equation is:
            </p>
            <md>y = mx + c</md>
            <ol>
                <li>Make the horizontal coordinate (<m>x</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                <li>Find <m>x</m> when <m>y = 20</m>, <m>m = 3</m>, and <m>c = 5</m>.</li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <md>y = mx + c</md>
                    <md>y - c = mx</md>
                    <md>x = \frac{y - c}{m}</md>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <md>x = \frac{20 - 5}{3}</md>
                    <md>x = \frac{15}{3}</md>
                    <md>x = 5</md>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The total geometric area (<m>A</m>) enclosed within a trapezoid depends on its perpendicular vertical height (<m>h</m>) and the lengths of its two parallel horizontal bases (<m>a</m> and <m>b</m>). The mathematical relationship is:
                </p>
                <md>A = \frac{1}{2}(a + b)h</md>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make the vertical height (<m>h</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>h</m> when <m>A = 30</m>, <m>a = 4</m>, and <m>b = 6</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><term>(a)</term> <md>h = \frac{2A}{a + b}</md></p>
                <p><term>(b)</term> <md>h = \frac{2(30)}{4 + 6} = \frac{60}{10} = 6</md></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In banking and finance, the flat simple interest earned (<m>I</m>) on a financial investment depends on the initial principal money deposited (<m>P</m>), the annual interest percentage rate (<m>R</m>), and the overall accumulation time in years (<m>T</m>). The expression is:
                </p>
                <md>I = \frac{PRT}{100}</md>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make the annual interest percentage rate (<m>R</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>R</m> when <m>I = 200</m>, <m>P = 1000</m>, and <m>T = 4</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><term>(a)</term> <md>R = \frac{100I}{PT}</md></p>
                <p><term>(b)</term> <md>R = \frac{100(200)}{1000 \times 4} = \frac{20000}{4000} = 5</md></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An industrial factory produces two distinct categories of parts daily, where <m>x</m> represents the quantity of standard components produced and <m>y</m> represents the quantity of premium components produced. Their balanced production constraint relationship is given by:
                </p>
                <md>3y + 2x = 12</md>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make the premium component quantity (<m>y</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>y</m> when standard components produced <m>x = 3</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><term>(a)</term> <md>y = \frac{12 - 2x}{3}</md></p>
                <p><term>(b)</term> <md>y = \frac{12 - 2(3)}{3} = \frac{12 - 6}{3} = \frac{6}{3} = 2</md></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The physical volumetric capacity (<m>V</m>) inside a perfect right cylinder is calculated using the constant ratio pi (<m>\pi</m>), the cylinder base radius (<m>r</m>), and the total vertical height (<m>h</m>). The formula is:
                </p>
                <md>V = \pi r^2 h</md>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make the vertical height (<m>h</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>h</m> when <m>V = 154</m>, <m>r = 7</m>, and <m>\pi \approx \frac{22}{7}</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><term>(a)</term> <md>h = \frac{V}{\pi r^2}</md></p>
                <p><term>(b)</term> <md>h = \frac{154}{\frac{22}{7} \times 49} = \frac{154}{22 \times 7} = \frac{154}{154} = 1</md></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    To convert meteorological data, the ambient outdoor temperature reading in degrees Fahrenheit (<m>F</m>) is linked to its equivalent value in degrees Celsius (<m>C</m>) by the following scale equation:
                </p>
                <md>F = \frac{9}{5}C + 32</md>
                <ol>
                    <li>Make the Celsius reading (<m>C</m>) the subject of the formula.</li>
                    <li>Find <m>C</m> when the temperature reads <m>F = 68</m>.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><term>(a)</term> <md>C = \frac{5(F - 32)}{9}</md></p>
                <p><term>(b)</term> <md>C = \frac{5(68 - 32)}{9} = \frac{5(36)}{9} = 5 \times 4 = 20</md></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide numerical tracking sheets where letters are temporarily replaced with boxes, or use physical equation scales to demonstrate balancing inverse operations step-by-step.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Task learners with changing the subject of formulas where the target variable appears more than once (e.g., <m>A = P(1 + rt)</m> to make <m>P</m> or <m>t</m> the subject), introducing factoring strategies.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>
